{
  "abstract": "We prove that an imaginary quadratic field of fundamental discriminant $-D$ whose ordinary ideal class group has exponent dividing forty satisfies $\\log\\log D<40000$. Thus $B=\\exp(\\exp40000)$ and $\\log B=\\exp40000$. The argument improves the previous threshold $52000$ within the short-ideal method by changing the quartic-character estimate and its family summation.\n\nWe difference Gaussian sums along conductor-ideal sublattices. Their indices are ideal norms, and balanced shapes admit translations of length proportional to the square root of that norm. Exact finite Fourier inversion and Poisson summation give a completion bound whose shortest-vector term can be averaged over classes because the conductor ideals remain fixed. Exceptional shapes are charged separately. Error-function smoothing handles real infinite signs without treating them as periodic. A common Bessel weight preserves the induction identity in both signatures, including its small-scale mixture and norm tails.\n\nFor smaller auxiliary discriminants, either the conductor admits the required decomposition or its complementary small-prime product is bounded. The resulting complementary prime-count restriction and a weighted generating function control the failed decompositions. Larger auxiliary discriminants have a separate complementary-product restriction, treated with another Burgess moment. Retaining the ordinary-genus projector through translation and low-cutoff integration reduces two boundary family costs, with explicit unrestricted remainders. A complementary-conductor argument also strengthens the quadratic-genus lower count. Combining these estimates with the published quintic norm theorem gives the stated bound.\n\nThe proof specifies all imported analytic results and uses no census. Two reproducible certificates check 190 positive 256-bit interval inequalities and twenty-five exact identities, separately from the analytic arguments. This gives neither a classification nor an improvement to the strongest numerical class-number-one cutoff.",
